  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "Using the Euclidean algorithm, find the greatest common divisor of each of the following pairs of natural numbers:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 3,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$124,448$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$4$",
          "worked": "Repeated subtraction preserves the GCD. Where several identical subtractions occur, they are grouped below.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(124,76)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(48,76)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(48,28)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(20,28)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(20,8)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(4,8)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(4,4)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(124,448)&=4.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 164
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$342,639$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9$",
          "worked": "Repeated subtraction preserves the GCD. Group repeated subtractions of the same smaller number.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(342,297)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(45,297)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(45,27)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(18,27)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(18,9)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(9,9)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(342,639)&=9.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 148
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$522,234$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$18$",
          "worked": "Subtract $234$ twice from $522$, then subtract $54$ four times from $234$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(522,234)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(54,234)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(522,234)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(54,18)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(522,234)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(18,18)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(522,234)&=18.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$963,396$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$9$",
          "worked": "Repeated subtraction preserves the GCD. Group repeated subtractions of the same smaller number.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(963,396)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(171,396)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(963,396)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(171,54)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(963,396)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(9,54)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(963,396)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(9,9)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(963,396)&=9.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$616,737$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$11$",
          "worked": "Subtract $616$ from $737$, then subtract $121$ five times from $616$. Continue subtracting $11$ from $121$ until both numbers are $11$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(616,737)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(616,121)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,737)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(11,121)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,737)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(11,11)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,737)&=11.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$616,858$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$22$",
          "worked": "Repeated subtraction preserves the GCD. Group repeated subtractions of the same smaller number.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(616,242)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(132,242)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(132,110)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(22,110)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=\\operatorname{GCD}(22,22)\\\\\\operatorname{GCD}(616,858)&=22.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 132
      }
    ]
  },
